Q1. Deadlock Debugging (10 Marks)
Deadlock Condition
Process P1 locks R1.
Process P2 locks R2.
P1 requests R2, but it is already locked by P2.
P2 requests R1, but it is already locked by P1.
Both processes wait indefinitely for each other.
This creates a Circular Wait, one of the four necessary conditions for deadlock.
The deadlock occurs because:
Mutual Exclusion exists.
Hold and Wait exists.
No Preemption exists.
Circular Wait exists.
Optimization
Follow a fixed resource allocation order.
Example: Both P1 and P2 should always lock R1 first and then R2.
Alternatively, use try-lock with timeout and release resources if the second lock is unavailable.
Apply deadlock prevention by breaking the Circular Wait condition.
This ensures both processes execute without deadlock while keeping the process logic unchanged.


Q2. CPU Utilization Problem (10 Marks)
Cause of Low CPU Utilization
CPU utilization is only 30%, although processes are available.
Scheduler log shows repeated transitions:
Ready → Waiting → Ready → Waiting.
Processes spend most of their time waiting for I/O.
The workload is I/O-bound, not CPU-bound.
CPU remains idle while processes wait for I/O completion.
Frequent context switches also waste CPU time.
System-Level Optimization
Increase the degree of multiprogramming so another process runs during I/O waits.
Use asynchronous or buffered I/O to reduce waiting time.
Improve disk performance using SSDs or caching.
Use an efficient scheduling algorithm that quickly selects ready processes.
Balance CPU-bound and I/O-bound processes to improve CPU utilization.


Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging (10 Marks)
Memory Issue
Program has:
Code = 5 MB
Heap = 150 MB
Stack = 1 MB per thread
Threads = 25
Total stack memory = 25 MB.
Frequent page faults indicate memory pages are repeatedly moved between RAM and secondary storage.
Large heap and multiple active threads increase memory accesses.
Continuous page swapping causes performance degradation.
This results in increased page fault handling time and reduced execution speed.
Optimizations
Reduce heap memory usage by releasing unused objects and avoiding unnecessary allocations.
Use a thread pool instead of creating many threads.
Increase physical RAM if possible.
Improve memory locality to reduce page faults.
Use efficient memory allocation techniques to minimize fragmentation.


Q4. Starvation Debugging (10 Marks)
Root Cause
Scheduler uses a Multilevel Queue.
Queue Priority:
Q1 – Interactive (Highest)
Q2 – System
Q3 – Batch (Lowest)
Process P5 belongs to Q3.
Continuous arrival of processes in Q1 and Q2 prevents Q3 from executing.
Since Q3 follows FCFS, P5 keeps waiting indefinitely.
This condition is called Starvation (Indefinite Blocking).
Optimization
Implement Aging, which gradually increases the priority of long-waiting processes.
Allocate a fixed percentage of CPU time to Q3.
Use Multilevel Feedback Queue (MLFQ) instead of a fixed Multilevel Queue.
Periodically promote waiting batch processes to higher-priority queues.
These techniques ensure fairness and prevent starvation.


Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging (10 Marks)
Cause
Server performs 20,000 context switches per second.
Time quantum = 1 ms.
Average CPU burst = 2 ms.
Threads are preempted before completing execution.
Very frequent I/O operations force processes into waiting state.
Every preemption and I/O completion causes another context switch.
CPU spends significant time saving and restoring process states instead of executing tasks.
This increases scheduling overhead and reduces system performance.
Optimizations
Increase the time quantum (e.g., 4–5 ms) to reduce unnecessary preemption.
Reduce I/O frequency using asynchronous I/O, buffering, or batching.
Optimize thread scheduling to minimize excessive switching.
Reduce the number of active threads using thread pools.
Balance CPU-bound and I/O-bound workloads to improve overall efficiency.
